-- ownership: a record is a cell (`ownership-checker.md` §31.2). A record
-- literal's object is `site(ι)`, and a record type no longer crosses: a
-- record of numbers carries a cell. The record cells are the record
-- demand's (§31.3) and reach summaries with §31.7; no list's fact reads
-- them, so `distance` still uses nothing a list can be, and `literal`
-- writes the list it put in a record, accepted `fresh`, as before.
--
-- An update's result is its base's object, written: `(ε, c ∖ F)`. For a
-- closed type its other fields are split out as before (`closed`); for an
-- open one the exception set says the written field is no longer the
-- base's, so `open`'s write of `s.items` writes only the new list, accepted
-- `fresh`, where the whole base kept before would hold `r.items` too.
type alias Point =
    x : Int
    y : Int


type alias Box =
    items : List Int
    count : Int


pub distance : Point → Int
distance p = p.x + p.y


pub literal : List Int → Int
literal xs =
    box = { items = [ 1 ], count = 0 }
    List.length [ …box.items, 2 ] + List.length xs


pub closed : Box → Int
closed b =
    s = { b | items = [] }
    List.length [ …s.items, 1 ] + List.length b.items


pub open : { r | items : List Int } → Int
open r =
    s = { r | items = [] }
    List.length [ …s.items, 1 ] + List.length r.items
